{"kind":"request","text":"Builder: Hosted coding authority and lifecycle contract. Implementation work package 1 of the reviewed connected-story design (planning b5f1fb3f, exact Draft2 evidence209a5ec9, approvalfc77cf82 and adopted direction0f358e52). Source baseline e6e6782 and the active acts/MCP a199 composition are context, not approval; reconcile current reviewed main, relevant existing requests and ecbc722a runner changes before editing.\n\nScope and decisions:\nDefine and implement the contract types for hosted coding and device continuity in docs/protocol.md, packages/contract and the affected roster/client transport types. The current Room rules already provide member keys, invitations, delegates, lanes, fork credentials, proposal review and landing; this adds resource-specific Agent authorization, admin-authorized registration of a separate role-agent member and its named controlling human, durable task/input/control receipts, coding operation IDs and lifecycle/status projections. Implement the authenticated Room-to-Agent boundary only against the reviewed contract; dependent runtime work must not invent authority from a viewer handle or website sign-in.\nDecide the exact signed request domain, room/agent/resource/action/body binding, expiry, nonce/replay behavior, session renewal and trusted service-binding result. Keep controller ownership immutable initially. Owner/admin control start, steer, pause, resume and cancel; authorized Room readers see sanitized activity and Room outcomes; raw transcripts, uncommitted files and command artifacts are owner/admin resources. Recheck current key/member/role/delegation at control and tool boundaries and revoke streams/sessions. Input acknowledgement follows durable receipt; repeats of an ID recover the same receipt and conflicting bytes are refused. Conversation input, applied steering and admitted Room acts remain separate records. Lifecycle distinguishes waiting, active, attention, paused, cancelled-pending, reconciliation, proposal, landed and published-through; expiry does not prove an external effect absent.\nPreserve declared act binding, R-IDEM exact-byte replay, R-ADM key-bound delegation, R-REV evidence/reservation behavior and existing Git/Room cleanup owners. A separate agent member makes independent human review possible; do not silently count a delegated human identity as a distinct author. Finalize renewal policy in active/waiting/review/paused states and retention deadlines in this contract.\nApply the reconciled design decisions, not Draft1 wording: existing sessions are read-only; controls use signed resource/action-bound current owner/admin requests. Initially one new agent member and one DO/conversation/lane per task; no in-place owner reassignment. Last-active-owner-key revocation or owner removal stops new tools/broker writes/renewals; another active owner device keeps independent-agent work alive. Admins retain private export/settlement, and lost-key recovery restores only an active member, never a removed handle. Optional delegated agent mode is not required in initial runtime delivery; its key-bound grantor rules and conditional acceptance remain specified. Render repository/command output inert in the key-owning origin and expose only minimal activity to other Room readers.\nFinalize explicit state and retry types for project payload/Git closure→immutable integrity manifest→epoch/instance/base guarded durable receipt plus current pointer→ack, old callback settlement without current-state replacement, linked new attempts retaining the first unknown duty, and expired/unavailable versus unknown operation results. Define checkpoint/export purge-root expiry and unresolved-effect pins. Pause/attention stop renewals; awaiting review renews only within visible configured window/budget.\nPrivate-read contract decision carried from independent revised-design review: initial raw owner/admin resources require a direct device read session, whose direct-key provenance and current role/resource are verified by the trusted Room boundary. authenticateRead returning an owner/admin member handle is insufficient. Deny delegation-issued read sessions raw resources by default; any separately scoped private-read capability is later explicit contract work. Sessions stay read-only, and controls remain separately signed.\nSource scope for the trusted boundary also includes packages/room/src/requests.ts, its HTTPS/RPC routing and session-authority projection, plus matching client wire helpers; do not leave the authorization behavior as types alone. Review/adopt the exact amendment before integrating runtime endpoints. Carry the saved dated plan and its commissioning index into your isolated implementation checkout as planning evidence, preserving the reviewed text and the private-read clarification; publish ordinary exact-head source artifacts for eventual main integration.\n\nDependencies and order:\nReviewed connected-story design; reconcile current acts/client composition and test-overhead changes. No provider operation is needed for contract review. Highest implementation priority remains test-overhead ecbc722a, followed by acts and builder-identified Jam blockers; this request adds no first-Jam readiness gate. Report source/design discrepancies before weakening scope.\n\nAcceptance outcome:\nA qualified owner/admin controls only the named resource; a fresh unauthorized browser, foreign owner, forged binding or stale/revoked key cannot read private workspace data or dispatch commands. Same-member active keys retain authority after one device is revoked. Input/control retry returns one durable result. One human-device revocation leaves an independently authorized agent running; if the optional delegated mode is selected, a revoked grantor makes that agent stop and require explicit replacement authority. Contract examples and types agree with current Room rules. Independent review of the exact amendment precedes dependent implementation. A read-only delegated session cannot control the agent. Separate owner-removal and last-active-key cases prove controller pause even while the independent agent member remains active; single-device revocation with another active key preserves work.\n\nValidation:\nKnown existing runners: npm run typecheck; npm run test:node --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-room; npm run test:node --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-client; npm run test:workerd --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-room for the real authority/session boundary. Use affected-file filters and the minimized workflow retained by ecbc722a; name exact commands in your promise after source reconciliation. Contract examples may be checked without a runtime suite. One final retained invariant gate at delivered head, no whole compile per case.\n\nDelivery: use an isolated request branch/worktree, record promise and exact source/probe evidence, publish every changed path at the exact delivered head, and obtain independent normal review before integration. Source code/protocol amendments are not authorized by the evidence-only design verdict alone. No numerical test inventory/per-guard sweep, no new first-Jam readiness gate. If a provider/storage/authority mechanism cannot meet the stated story, report a design discrepancy rather than silently reducing scope.","body":{"conditions":"A qualified owner/admin controls only the named resource; a fresh unauthorized browser, foreign owner, forged binding or stale/revoked key cannot read private workspace data or dispatch commands. Same-member active keys retain authority after one device is revoked. Input/control retry returns one durable result. One human-device revocation leaves an independently authorized agent running; if the optional delegated mode is selected, a revoked grantor makes that agent stop and require explicit replacement authority. Contract examples and types agree with current Room rules. Independent review of the exact amendment precedes dependent implementation. A read-only delegated session cannot control the agent. Separate owner-removal and last-active-key cases prove controller pause even while the independent agent member remains active; single-device revocation with another active key preserves work. Relevant contract decisions and exact implementation evidence independently reviewed; source artifacts integrated through normal landing. Preserve named lifecycle/authority/durability rules and existing owed work. Known existing runners: npm run typecheck; npm run test:node --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-room; npm run test:node --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-client; npm run test:workerd --workspace @generalbusiness/artroom-room for the real authority/session boundary. Use affected-file filters and the minimized workflow retained by ecbc722a; name exact commands in your promise after source reconciliation. Contract examples may be checked without a runtime suite. One final retained invariant gate at delivered head, no whole compile per case.","dependencies":"Reviewed connected-story design; reconcile current acts/client composition and test-overhead changes. No provider operation is needed for contract review. Highest implementation priority remains test-overhead ecbc722a, followed by acts and builder-identified Jam blockers; this request adds no first-Jam readiness gate. Report source/design discrepancies before weakening scope.","priority":"After highest-priority test-overhead ecbc722a; preserve acts/Jam-blocker/Jam-and-docs order. No new first-Jam gate.","target_head":"e6e6782830e0ca8a68f0d11c4d4ece5e4e98c967","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}